base('exp-derivative','Derivative and positivity of the exponential',['t and C are real; C is constant.'],'e^(−Ct)>0 and its derivative is −Ce^(−Ct).','This standard exponential-function fact remains a boundary. A derivation depends on how exp is defined, for example by a power series or as an inverse of an integral-defined logarithm.','Standard theorem boundary')
base('extreme-value','Extreme-value theorem on a closed interval',['h is continuous on a finite closed interval [a,b].'],'h attains both its maximum and minimum on [a,b].','This is a completeness-based theorem of real analysis. It is not implied by differentiability rules alone. Compactness of [a,b] is one route to its proof, left as the next explicit boundary.','Standard theorem boundary')
add('stationary-extremum','Derivative at an interior extremum',['h is differentiable at an interior local maximum c.'],'h′(c)=0.','For small positive t, [h(c+t)−h(c)]/t≤0; for small negative t the quotient is ≥0. Since both sides tend to the same derivative, it is both ≤0 and ≥0, hence zero. At a local minimum reverse the signs.',['derivative-def','limit-rules'])
add('rolle','Rolle’s theorem',['h is continuous on [a,b], differentiable on (a,b), a<b, and h(a)=h(b).'],'There exists c∈(a,b) with h′(c)=0.','If h is constant, every interior derivative is zero. Otherwise some value differs from the common endpoint value. A maximum or minimum attained by the extreme-value theorem must then occur in the interior. The stationary-extremum result gives zero derivative there.',['extreme-value','stationary-extremum'])
add('mean-value','Derive the mean value theorem',['h is continuous on [a,b], differentiable on (a,b), and a<b.'],'There is c∈(a,b) with h(b)−h(a)=h′(c)(b−a).','Set m=(h(b)−h(a))/(b−a) and g(x)=h(x)−m(x−a). The endpoints of g agree. Rolle gives g′(c)=0, hence h′(c)=m. Multiply by b−a. Differentiation of the linear term follows directly from the derivative definition.',['rolle','derivative-def','limit-rules'])
